## Brute Force Approach

⏳ Complexity

Time: O(n²) → all pairs

Space: O(1) → no extra memory


class Solution {
    public int maxArea(int[] height) {

        // This variable will store the maximum area found so far.
        // We start with 0 because area cannot be negative.
        int maxarea = 0;

        // Outer loop: picks the first line of the container
        // i represents the left boundary of the container
        for (int i = 0; i < height.length; i++) {

            // Inner loop: picks the second line of the container
            // j represents the right boundary (always ahead of i)
            for (int j = i + 1; j < height.length; j++) {

                // The height of water is limited by the shorter line.
                // Water will spill over the taller line if the shorter one is small.
                int h = Math.min(height[i], height[j]);

                // Width of the container is the distance between the two indices
                // Since j > i, width is always positive
                int width = j - i;

                // Area is calculated using:
                // area = height of water * width between the lines
                int area = h * width;

                // Update maxarea if the current area is larger
                maxarea = Math.max(maxarea, area);
            }
        }

        // After checking all possible pairs of lines,
        // return the maximum area found
        return maxarea;
    }
}

## Optimal Approach

⏱ Complexity

Time: O(n) → each pointer moves at most n times

Space: O(1) → no extra memory used

class Solution {
    public int maxArea(int[] height) {

        // maxarea stores the maximum water area found so far.
        // Initialized to 0 because area cannot be negative.
        int maxarea = 0;

        // Two pointers:
        // i starts at the beginning (leftmost line)
        // j starts at the end (rightmost line)
        int i = 0, j = height.length - 1;

        // Continue while the two pointers do not cross
        while (i < j) {

            // The height of water is limited by the shorter line.
            // Even if one line is taller, water will overflow from the shorter one.
            int h = Math.min(height[i], height[j]);

            // Width is the horizontal distance between the two lines
            int w = j - i;

            // Area formed by the current pair of lines
            // area = height * width
            int area = w * h;

            // Update maxarea if the current area is larger
            maxarea = Math.max(area, maxarea);

            // Move the pointer that has the smaller height.
            // Reason:
            // The area is limited by the shorter line.
            // Moving the taller line will not increase the height,
            // but moving the shorter line may find a taller boundary
            // and potentially increase the area.
            if (height[i] <= height[j]) {
                i++;   // move left pointer inward
            } else {
                j--;   // move right pointer inward
            }
        }

        // System.gc() is unnecessary here.
        // Java's garbage collector runs automatically when needed.
        // Calling it manually does not improve performance.
        System.gc();

        // Return the maximum area found
        return maxarea;
    }
}
